PART 2 — WHAT IS IT
[SCREEN: Switch to Slide 1]
"So what is this actually. A Routine is just a prompt that runs on its own. You write out what you want Claude to do — same as you'd normally talk to it. You connect whatever accounts it needs access to. You pick when you want it to run. And that's it. It just goes."
"There are three ways to kick it off. You can schedule it — every morning, every hour, every weekday, whatever. Minimum is once per hour. You can also trigger it from another tool, so if something happens somewhere else it fires this automatically. Or you can set up a webhook — so for example when a new lead comes in, it immediately runs and does something with that lead."
"And the big thing here — it runs on Anthropic's servers. Not yours. So your laptop doesn't need to be on. You don't need anything running in the background. You just wake up and whatever you set it to do is already done."

PART 3 — OLD WAY VS NEW WAY
[SCREEN: Switch to Slide 2]
"Okay so why does this matter. Because I know a lot of people watching this already have n8n or Make set up and are thinking — I already have automations, why do I need this."
"Here's the honest answer. Building something in n8n looks like this — you open it up, you start dragging nodes around, connecting them together, mapping your variables, setting up credentials for every single service. You test it, something breaks, the error message tells you nothing useful, you spend an hour figuring out what went wrong. And when it finally works your server has to stay running or the whole thing stops."
"I've done that. It works. But it's slow to build and annoying to maintain."
"With Routines you just write out what you want to happen. Claude figures out the steps. And the thing that actually makes this better — if something goes wrong halfway through, Claude reads the error, adjusts, and keeps going. n8n just stops. Claude doesn't."
"The only limitation is if a tool you want to use doesn't have an API — like if you need Claude to actually log into a website and click around. That won't work. Every run starts completely fresh, no saved logins, nothing like that. But anything with an API works fine."

